(5844089, 848552, 47541, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>First impressions are that this is a great shot. Composition is fantastic, DOF perfect, the B&amp;W processing is spot on. The one thing that stands out as holding it back is the tat''s at the right seem to be such a main element. This is of course something that the subjects and those that know them will love but for DPC I suspect a lot of voters saw that as a negative.<br/> <br/>This deserved a 6.x and it got that. I think it deserved higher but that''s just my opinion.<br/> <br/>This is great work, keep it up.<br/>Gerry</td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club First impressions are that this is a great shot. Composition is fantastic, DOF perfect, the B&W processing is spot on. The one thing that stands out as holding it back is the tat''s at the right seem to be such a main element. This is of course something that the subjects and those that know them will love but for DPC I suspect a lot of voters saw that as a negative. This deserved a 6.x and it got that. I think it deserved higher but that''s just my opinion. This is great work, keep it up.Gerry', '2010-01-30 00:01:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844092, 848772, 77196, '<td valign="top">Great lighting and nice detail, but placing the horizon in the middle of the flower creates some major problems with this. There is nice contrast between the flower and the green trees, and very little contrast between the flower and the sky. Best of challenges are typically voted pretty critically. I''d focus on the fact that you got almost all 4''s &amp; 5''s, with a nice number of 6''s and a few higher scores. <br/></td>', 'Great lighting and nice detail, but placing the horizon in the middle of the flower creates some major problems with this. There is nice contrast between the flower and the green trees, and very little contrast between the flower and the sky. Best of challenges are typically voted pretty critically. I''d focus on the fact that you got almost all 4''s & 5''s, with a nice number of 6''s and a few higher scores. ', '2010-01-30 00:06:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844093, 847768, 106543, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club! <br/> <br/>First Impression: </b> Two lightning bolts in one shot! I especially liked the lightning strike near the church, it looks like it is lighting all of the front. Or maybe it is.<br/> <br/><b>Subject: </b> It''s not often that two lightning strikes are captured it a photo, at least not this well done. Best of 2009 and maybe a long time before you get a chance to capture this again.<br/> <br/><b>Technical: </b> At first I felt there was too much space at the very top, after looking at it though I think it''s perfect. If you take any away from the top you would lose some of that blue colour.<br/> <br/><b>Final thoughts: </b> I don''t really see anyway that you could improve this particular image. I hope to see more from you on DPC. I checked my favourite photographers by votes given, over 9 challenges I''ve given you an average of 6.67.<br/> <br/>If you have any questions <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/messaging.php?action=compose&amp;USER_ID=106543" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">click here to message me</a>! <br/> <br/>Senay<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2010-01-30 00:58:12.</i></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  First Impression:  Two lightning bolts in one shot! I especially liked the lightning strike near the church, it looks like it is lighting all of the front. Or maybe it is. Subject:  It''s not often that two lightning strikes are captured it a photo, at least not this well done. Best of 2009 and maybe a long time before you get a chance to capture this again. Technical:  At first I felt there was too much space at the very top, after looking at it though I think it''s perfect. If you take any away from the top you would lose some of that blue colour. Final thoughts:  I don''t really see anyway that you could improve this particular image. I hope to see more from you on DPC. I checked my favourite photographers by votes given, over 9 challenges I''ve given you an average of 6.67. If you have any questions click here to message me!  Senay', '2010-01-30 00:06:58.000000', '2010-01-30 00:58:12.000000', 0),
(5844098, 847238, 47541, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>First impressions are that this is a great study in lines and geometry.<br/> <br/>The HDR processing is good, and it works well. (I see you like the HDR look). The textures really show through. There is a little bit of noise in the sky that I''d prefer to have been dealt to.<br/> <br/>Composition is good with great leading lines drawing us to the top of the tower.<br/> <br/>I can''t anything else to fault in the shot and the voters couldn''t either with a solid 6.x score. Well done.<br/>Gerry<br/></td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club First impressions are that this is a great study in lines and geometry. The HDR processing is good, and it works well. (I see you like the HDR look). The textures really show through. There is a little bit of noise in the sky that I''d prefer to have been dealt to. Composition is good with great leading lines drawing us to the top of the tower. I can''t anything else to fault in the shot and the voters couldn''t either with a solid 6.x score. Well done.Gerry', '2010-01-30 00:13:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844101, 848725, 77196, '<td valign="top">This shot is one of the reasons I will probably never score well on dpchallenge. This is a great capture. The OOF hints that the adult may feel the boy''s confidence may not be wholly earned - it adds a bit of surrealness to the shot - something true, but hard to believe. There is great congruency between the boy''s expression, head &amp; eye position and the title. You can put your bat and ball away. You''ve hit this one out of the park.<br/>BTW-check out the comment from Posthumous - he don''t give out ones like that very often. <br/><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2010-01-30 00:18:50.</i></td>', 'This shot is one of the reasons I will probably never score well on dpchallenge. This is a great capture. The OOF hints that the adult may feel the boy''s confidence may not be wholly earned - it adds a bit of surrealness to the shot - something true, but hard to believe. There is great congruency between the boy''s expression, head & eye position and the title. You can put your bat and ball away. You''ve hit this one out of the park.BTW-check out the comment from Posthumous - he don''t give out ones like that very often. ', '2010-01-30 00:16:31.000000', '2010-01-30 00:18:50.000000', 0),
(5844104, 848507, 77196, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the comments. In retrospect, this probably would have done much better if I''d darkened the curtains to black. <br/>This was a shot of a live performance, and was one of the back row dancers in a group of about a dozen. The River Dance Company is dedicated in using dance in Christian worship, so think "praising God" as a context for the picture. <br/>I loved the expression on her face. <br/></td>', 'Thanks for the comments. In retrospect, this probably would have done much better if I''d darkened the curtains to black. This was a shot of a live performance, and was one of the back row dancers in a group of about a dozen. The River Dance Company is dedicated in using dance in Christian worship, so think "praising God" as a context for the picture. I loved the expression on her face. ', '2010-01-30 00:23:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844126, 848776, 30982, '<td valign="top">I just really like this!!</td>', 'I just really like this!!', '2010-01-30 00:48:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844129, 847658, 76335, '<td valign="top">I KNEW this was yours -- I almost called you out by name! No idea what you''re doing down here in the trenches, but I guess I''m not too surprised. Nine from me. Excellent! Don''t stop doing what you''re doing.</td>', 'I KNEW this was yours -- I almost called you out by name! No idea what you''re doing down here in the trenches, but I guess I''m not too surprised. Nine from me. Excellent! Don''t stop doing what you''re doing.', '2010-01-30 00:50:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844131, 847828, 106543, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club! <br/> <br/>First Impression: </b> I gave this a 7. I like the processing and looks great in black and white.<br/> <br/><b>Composition: </b> Horizon line seems to be in the middle, but it''s not as noticeable with the house in the fore front. I might have taken a little bit off the bottom and left sides though.<br/> <br/><b>Subject: </b> Abandoned house in the middle of nowhere is great, title adds to the mystery. Only way it could be better if there was a hooded figure somewhere in the picture.<br/> <br/><b>Technical: </b> Win/Lose situation on the processing it seems, some seem to like the PP others think it''s too much. I wouldn''t change anything you did though. B/w was a good choice too.<br/> <br/><b>Final thoughts: </b> Like I said I gave this a 7, I thought it would do much better, but DPC is tricky. Looking at your other challenge entries it looks like this type of PP has worked for you before. I think you were just hit by HDR haters.<br/> <br/>If you have any questions <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/messaging.php?action=compose&amp;USER_ID=106543" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">click here to message me</a>! <br/> <br/>Senay<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2010-01-30 00:58:30.</i></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  First Impression:  I gave this a 7. I like the processing and looks great in black and white. Composition:  Horizon line seems to be in the middle, but it''s not as noticeable with the house in the fore front. I might have taken a little bit off the bottom and left sides though. Subject:  Abandoned house in the middle of nowhere is great, title adds to the mystery. Only way it could be better if there was a hooded figure somewhere in the picture. Technical:  Win/Lose situation on the processing it seems, some seem to like the PP others think it''s too much. I wouldn''t change anything you did though. B/w was a good choice too. Final thoughts:  Like I said I gave this a 7, I thought it would do much better, but DPC is tricky. Looking at your other challenge entries it looks like this type of PP has worked for you before. I think you were just hit by HDR haters. If you have any questions click here to message me!  Senay', '2010-01-30 00:51:00.000000', '2010-01-30 00:58:30.000000', 0),
(5844138, 847036, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Great detail and that stare is awesome - you did a fine job of catching that look!  Solid composition, drawing us to that very focused eye, then gradually letting us take in the sneer and the laid back ears.  Color is good, but that bright green is just a bit off-putting; while it is undoubtedly the color that was there, the mind (at least mine) almost wants a darker, "meaner" green.  The green that is there is too kind and gentle for the cat''s stare, if that makes sense.  (And it probably doesn''t!)  Although you''d lose the marvelous golden tones of the eye and the coat, I do wonder what this might look like in monochrome.  And yep, I think the Topaz did a nice job of bringing out the detail without being obviously Topaz''d - I''d not have known it without your notes.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Great detail and that stare is awesome - you did a fine job of catching that look!  Solid composition, drawing us to that very focused eye, then gradually letting us take in the sneer and the laid back ears.  Color is good, but that bright green is just a bit off-putting; while it is undoubtedly the color that was there, the mind (at least mine) almost wants a darker, "meaner" green.  The green that is there is too kind and gentle for the cat''s stare, if that makes sense.  (And it probably doesn''t!)  Although you''d lose the marvelous golden tones of the eye and the coat, I do wonder what this might look like in monochrome.  And yep, I think the Topaz did a nice job of bringing out the detail without being obviously Topaz''d - I''d not have known it without your notes.', '2010-01-30 01:01:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844139, 848022, 47541, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>First impressions are that it is a nice shot with a quirky aspect to it.<br/> <br/>The title is a great choice as it makes us think about what the picture is showing us - it draws us in to spending more time looking at it.<br/> <br/>The lighting is good (I wish I could use speedlights as well) but that lamp is out of place in the scene being all yellow and bright and all. A bit of dodge/burn to lessen the impact of some of the busy elements in the background would have been helpful.<br/> <br/>I''m surprised this didn''t make a 6.x score but I guess that is more to do with it not having a "wow" factor for DPC than any real reflection on the shot itself.<br/> <br/>This is a great shot and even if DPC didn''t vote it as high as it should have been it makes a wonderful reminder of this time that women only experience a few times in their life.<br/> <br/>Good work<br/>Gerry</td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club First impressions are that it is a nice shot with a quirky aspect to it. The title is a great choice as it makes us think about what the picture is showing us - it draws us in to spending more time looking at it. The lighting is good (I wish I could use speedlights as well) but that lamp is out of place in the scene being all yellow and bright and all. A bit of dodge/burn to lessen the impact of some of the busy elements in the background would have been helpful. I''m surprised this didn''t make a 6.x score but I guess that is more to do with it not having a "wow" factor for DPC than any real reflection on the shot itself. This is a great shot and even if DPC didn''t vote it as high as it should have been it makes a wonderful reminder of this time that women only experience a few times in their life. Good workGerry', '2010-01-30 01:02:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844141, 848819, 76335, '<td valign="top">I bumped this to an eight at the last minute, Joe. Should have guessed it was yours. Really good.</td>', 'I bumped this to an eight at the last minute, Joe. Should have guessed it was yours. Really good.', '2010-01-30 01:03:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844145, 848329, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Hi Alec,<br/>Looking at your portfolio, you have a wonderful talent for capturing people - this is one such example.  Excellent focus on his eyes - and a whole world seems to be reflected in them - and nice use of shallow DOF.  My only suggestion would be to crop the post on the right.  While keeping it in maintains your aspect ratio (and I tend to like to do that), taking it out removes an unneeded element from the photo.  <br/> <br/>As it is, this scored pretty well for a "Best of" challenge and was well appreciated by your commenters.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Hi Alec,Looking at your portfolio, you have a wonderful talent for capturing people - this is one such example.  Excellent focus on his eyes - and a whole world seems to be reflected in them - and nice use of shallow DOF.  My only suggestion would be to crop the post on the right.  While keeping it in maintains your aspect ratio (and I tend to like to do that), taking it out removes an unneeded element from the photo.   As it is, this scored pretty well for a "Best of" challenge and was well appreciated by your commenters.', '2010-01-30 01:10:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844151, 848554, 62357, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>I am sorry you didn''t score higher for your Best of 2009 shot, but I think the Best of Challenges are very hard to score well in.<br/> <br/>I think there are a number of things you could have done to improve the image and increase your score in the challenge.   Starting with the assumption that you had the time to do this (and chances are you due to the nature of the shot) I will mention the things you could have done at the time of shooting, then the post processing steps.<br/> <br/>I believe a shallower depth of field would have enabled you to really isolate the biker from the background.  This I feel would have given much more impact.  There are parts of the back that blend into the trees which may have been avoided.  Somebody else already mentioned this, but I will say it again, maybe crouching down and taking this from a lower point of view would also have helped isolate the bike.  By the way, I think it is awesome that you captured the shadow of the bike so clearly.  it really is a bonus.  <br/> <br/>As far as composition goes, I''m afraid I do find the placement of the bike to be a little too centered.  Unless you were about to step into an abyss, I think a few steps to the left when you were shooting would have resulted in a slightly more interesting angle.  As I said, I am assuming you had the time to think of all these things, when I''m sure the reality was you saw the moment and just snapped quickly to make sure you got the moment.  (At least, that is what happens to me, I forget about everything technical in my quest to get the shot).<br/> <br/>When it comes to the post processing, again I think there are a few things that could have improved the final score.  As I mentioned above, the composition is a little bit too centered for my taste, so in this case I think cropping the image square could have helped a lot.  <br/> <br/>Additionally, the B &amp; W conversion could be better.  This feels a bit flat and could use a bit of a boost in contrast either via a levels or curves adjustment.  To be honest though, and remembering I haven''t seen the original, but I can''t help thinking this may have worked better in colour. (This coming from someone who mostly does B &amp; W)<br/> <br/>Anyway, I personally gave it a 6 during voting, but I think with slightly different processing it could have gone up to a 7.<br/> <br/>Feel free to pm me about this critique if you want.<br/> <br/>Sarah<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  I am sorry you didn''t score higher for your Best of 2009 shot, but I think the Best of Challenges are very hard to score well in. I think there are a number of things you could have done to improve the image and increase your score in the challenge.   Starting with the assumption that you had the time to do this (and chances are you due to the nature of the shot) I will mention the things you could have done at the time of shooting, then the post processing steps. I believe a shallower depth of field would have enabled you to really isolate the biker from the background.  This I feel would have given much more impact.  There are parts of the back that blend into the trees which may have been avoided.  Somebody else already mentioned this, but I will say it again, maybe crouching down and taking this from a lower point of view would also have helped isolate the bike.  By the way, I think it is awesome that you captured the shadow of the bike so clearly.  it really is a bonus.   As far as composition goes, I''m afraid I do find the placement of the bike to be a little too centered.  Unless you were about to step into an abyss, I think a few steps to the left when you were shooting would have resulted in a slightly more interesting angle.  As I said, I am assuming you had the time to think of all these things, when I''m sure the reality was you saw the moment and just snapped quickly to make sure you got the moment.  (At least, that is what happens to me, I forget about everything technical in my quest to get the shot). When it comes to the post processing, again I think there are a few things that could have improved the final score.  As I mentioned above, the composition is a little bit too centered for my taste, so in this case I think cropping the image square could have helped a lot.   Additionally, the B & W conversion could be better.  This feels a bit flat and could use a bit of a boost in contrast either via a levels or curves adjustment.  To be honest though, and remembering I haven''t seen the original, but I can''t help thinking this may have worked better in colour. (This coming from someone who mostly does B & W) Anyway, I personally gave it a 6 during voting, but I think with slightly different processing it could have gone up to a 7. Feel free to pm me about this critique if you want. Sarah', '2010-01-30 01:24:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844152, 848514, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>As I noted during voting, this shot has lovely natural light - you really took great advantage of it!  I find it interesting that your commenters seem split over the tree - some like it (or it doesn''t bother them), some don''t like it.  I''m in the "like it" camp - I think it works just fine here.  The colors are warm and natural, and I see from your notes that you didn''t do much to bring them out - nice light hand with the processing.  My only suggestion is as I noted during voting that it seems to be a bit oversharpened - this is visible mostly in the tree branches and it is incredibly hard to sharpen for web sized viewing without oversharpening tree branches.  Overall, I really do like this picture and am surprised it didn''t score higher than it did, but am pleased to see it made your personal top 5!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! As I noted during voting, this shot has lovely natural light - you really took great advantage of it!  I find it interesting that your commenters seem split over the tree - some like it (or it doesn''t bother them), some don''t like it.  I''m in the "like it" camp - I think it works just fine here.  The colors are warm and natural, and I see from your notes that you didn''t do much to bring them out - nice light hand with the processing.  My only suggestion is as I noted during voting that it seems to be a bit oversharpened - this is visible mostly in the tree branches and it is incredibly hard to sharpen for web sized viewing without oversharpening tree branches.  Overall, I really do like this picture and am surprised it didn''t score higher than it did, but am pleased to see it made your personal top 5!', '2010-01-30 01:33:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844156, 847450, 62357, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Sorry this did not score a bit higher for you, although I think a 6 is pretty OK in a Best of challenge.<br/> <br/>I like this image very much indeed.  There are lot of things to look at but it doesn''t feel busy. The rain adds to the mood very well.<br/> <br/>I personally have no problem whatsoever with the lens flare, it adds to the charm of this image.  The one thing that bothers me and it is really a minor issue and one I should be able to overcome, is the fact the monument is not square to the edge of the frame.  Also, the fact you have cut off the very corner edge of the monument base.  I know, I know.  I hate that things like that bother me (same with my own pics).  It''s such a nit picky thing, but I feel sure I am not the only anal retentive DPC voter.<br/> <br/>Sorry about that, I typically try to get beyond these silly minor things, but for some reason with this image it keeps coming back to me.  So silly.<br/> <br/>The B&amp;W conversion is wonderful.  I do really like the image, so sorry for the nit picky stuff.<br/> <br/>Sarah<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Sorry this did not score a bit higher for you, although I think a 6 is pretty OK in a Best of challenge. I like this image very much indeed.  There are lot of things to look at but it doesn''t feel busy. The rain adds to the mood very well. I personally have no problem whatsoever with the lens flare, it adds to the charm of this image.  The one thing that bothers me and it is really a minor issue and one I should be able to overcome, is the fact the monument is not square to the edge of the frame.  Also, the fact you have cut off the very corner edge of the monument base.  I know, I know.  I hate that things like that bother me (same with my own pics).  It''s such a nit picky thing, but I feel sure I am not the only anal retentive DPC voter. Sorry about that, I typically try to get beyond these silly minor things, but for some reason with this image it keeps coming back to me.  So silly. The B&W conversion is wonderful.  I do really like the image, so sorry for the nit picky stuff. Sarah', '2010-01-30 01:46:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844163, 848347, 62357, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>I''m afraid to say Clive, that this is going to prove mighty challenging on my part to give you a helpful critique.  I scored it a 9 and so it is hard to give you constructive criticism.<br/> <br/>You may want to ask somebody else to give you a critique later on.<br/> <br/>First thing that grabbed me was the lovely editing.  I don''t know what you did to it but it works well.  The soft muted tones are very appealing to me.  I imagine if it was shot in Egypt that the light maybe harsher than you have made it appear.  The very gentle vignette really focuses your attention to the centre.  <br/> <br/>Your signature square crop also works well for this as the whole frame is filled with interest, there is no space wasted.<br/> <br/>Not sure what you could have done to improve the score, it already got a pretty decent score in a Best of Challenge.<br/> <br/>If you want somebody to give a better critique, suggest you post a request in the critique club thread and someone else may add some value.<br/> <br/>Sarah</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  I''m afraid to say Clive, that this is going to prove mighty challenging on my part to give you a helpful critique.  I scored it a 9 and so it is hard to give you constructive criticism. You may want to ask somebody else to give you a critique later on. First thing that grabbed me was the lovely editing.  I don''t know what you did to it but it works well.  The soft muted tones are very appealing to me.  I imagine if it was shot in Egypt that the light maybe harsher than you have made it appear.  The very gentle vignette really focuses your attention to the centre.   Your signature square crop also works well for this as the whole frame is filled with interest, there is no space wasted. Not sure what you could have done to improve the score, it already got a pretty decent score in a Best of Challenge. If you want somebody to give a better critique, suggest you post a request in the critique club thread and someone else may add some value. Sarah', '2010-01-30 01:57:40.000000', NULL, 0),
