(5844501, 847060, 67343, '<td valign="top">Beautiful image love the lighting and composition.  Really does look like it belongs in a fairly tale. Congrats on your great finish.</td>', 'Beautiful image love the lighting and composition.  Really does look like it belongs in a fairly tale. Congrats on your great finish.', '2010-01-30 13:24:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844507, 847376, 106543, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club! <br/> <br/>First Impression: </b> I felt that it was a very nice portrait of a bride who must have been very happy when she received this!<br/> <br/><b>Composition: </b> Tilted angle works good, I like the line created by the dress and the black background.<br/> <br/><b>Technical: </b> The eyes are awesome! For the lighting maybe some more light could have been on the right side so that the hair does not blend with the background as much, although the lighting is great the way it is just a small detail.<br/> <br/><b>Final thoughts: </b> The only thing that could be done to improve this image would be to include all or more of her head.<br/> <br/>If you have any questions <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/messaging.php?action=compose&amp;USER_ID=106543" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">click here to message me</a>! <br/> <br/>Senay</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  First Impression:  I felt that it was a very nice portrait of a bride who must have been very happy when she received this! Composition:  Tilted angle works good, I like the line created by the dress and the black background. Technical:  The eyes are awesome! For the lighting maybe some more light could have been on the right side so that the hair does not blend with the background as much, although the lighting is great the way it is just a small detail. Final thoughts:  The only thing that could be done to improve this image would be to include all or more of her head. If you have any questions click here to message me!  Senay', '2010-01-30 13:46:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844513, 847856, 106543, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club! <br/> <br/>First Impression: </b> First thought was "NO NOT THE BEER!" then I read your title.<br/> <br/><b>Subject: </b> It seems that people had two feelings towards this picture, some thought it was interesting and others thought it lacked a certain something for the Best of 2009 challenge. Personally I thought it was a pretty cool idea! Being a fan of beer I really liked this shot, I gave it a 7.<br/> <br/><b>Technical: </b> The selective desaturation works well to highlight the beer taps. I''m not a big fan of how the angle, I think it would have worked better straight or fully diagonal.<br/> <br/><b>Final thoughts: </b> Now I''m thirsty, if it wasn''t 2:07 here I''d drink a beer.<br/> <br/>If you have any questions <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/messaging.php?action=compose&amp;USER_ID=106543" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">click here to message me</a>! <br/> <br/>Senay</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  First Impression:  First thought was "NO NOT THE BEER!" then I read your title. Subject:  It seems that people had two feelings towards this picture, some thought it was interesting and others thought it lacked a certain something for the Best of 2009 challenge. Personally I thought it was a pretty cool idea! Being a fan of beer I really liked this shot, I gave it a 7. Technical:  The selective desaturation works well to highlight the beer taps. I''m not a big fan of how the angle, I think it would have worked better straight or fully diagonal. Final thoughts:  Now I''m thirsty, if it wasn''t 2:07 here I''d drink a beer. If you have any questions click here to message me!  Senay', '2010-01-30 14:07:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844518, 848474, 109309, '<td valign="top">I didn''t Participate in this challenge, or vote, but I personally like this photo and would have voted it 8 or 9..... It is easily a favorite.</td>', 'I didn''t Participate in this challenge, or vote, but I personally like this photo and would have voted it 8 or 9..... It is easily a favorite.', '2010-01-30 14:19:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844520, 848896, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB</b> <br/> <br/>Jeepers, I wonder where this is!<br/>:-))<br/>Great shot, and I say that as one who has aimed a camera at this bridge many, many times in its many moods.<br/> <br/>You caught the personality of this bridge beautifully.  From the lighting to the delicate treatment of the fog bank that is about to envelop you, there it is.  I think it is very well done and you received many fine comments reinforcing this viewpoint. <br/> <br/>Because it''s my job to nitpic, if this was mine (and I know it isn''t) I might have toned down those lights at the other end of the bridge at the waterline.  They (IMO) detract just slightly from the overall majesty of your image.<br/> <br/>Good luck in your new job and your new city and come back to see us soon!<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB  Jeepers, I wonder where this is!:-))Great shot, and I say that as one who has aimed a camera at this bridge many, many times in its many moods. You caught the personality of this bridge beautifully.  From the lighting to the delicate treatment of the fog bank that is about to envelop you, there it is.  I think it is very well done and you received many fine comments reinforcing this viewpoint.  Because it''s my job to nitpic, if this was mine (and I know it isn''t) I might have toned down those lights at the other end of the bridge at the waterline.  They (IMO) detract just slightly from the overall majesty of your image. Good luck in your new job and your new city and come back to see us soon! Alice', '2010-01-30 14:22:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844527, 848786, 109309, '<td valign="top">I didn''t participate in this challenge nor did I vote. However, when I came upon this photo I was drawn in. It''s not technically perfect but there is so much emotion and feeling in it that It doesn''t matter. In a matter of a second this photo (at least for me) tells a story of a lifetime. This photo shows the human side of photography. It also says a lot to me that you chose this photo for your best of 2009, It tells me that he meant a lot to you and he will be missed....</td>', 'I didn''t participate in this challenge nor did I vote. However, when I came upon this photo I was drawn in. It''s not technically perfect but there is so much emotion and feeling in it that It doesn''t matter. In a matter of a second this photo (at least for me) tells a story of a lifetime. This photo shows the human side of photography. It also says a lot to me that you chose this photo for your best of 2009, It tells me that he meant a lot to you and he will be missed....', '2010-01-30 14:31:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844549, 848525, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB</b> <br/> <br/>Oh, what beautiful, icy trees.  They look so delicate and lacy it is almost possible to forget they are also a product of cold, cold weather.<br/> <br/>When I first looked at this lovely, icy scene, I too said to myself, "oh dear, that road."  But I''m not going to belabor the point as it''s been made a number of times in your comments.  <br/> <br/>You were using a moderately wide-angle lens, so unless the grounds were so inhospitable that you just couldn''t move around, sometimes taking many pics from many vantage points helps in the final choice.  Also, since this is Advanced editing, a little cloning of an unwanted car or two might come in handy.  <br/> <br/>But you were there, and I was not and you probably already know all this.<br/> <br/>There''s a pretty good tutorial on resizing here at DPC and if you use Photoshop, their "Save for Web" is a pretty good way of getting a useful size for competition.<br/> <br/>Good luck in your future entries in DPC.  You''ve got a good eye and I''m looking forward to seeing more of your work.<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB  Oh, what beautiful, icy trees.  They look so delicate and lacy it is almost possible to forget they are also a product of cold, cold weather. When I first looked at this lovely, icy scene, I too said to myself, "oh dear, that road."  But I''m not going to belabor the point as it''s been made a number of times in your comments.   You were using a moderately wide-angle lens, so unless the grounds were so inhospitable that you just couldn''t move around, sometimes taking many pics from many vantage points helps in the final choice.  Also, since this is Advanced editing, a little cloning of an unwanted car or two might come in handy.   But you were there, and I was not and you probably already know all this. There''s a pretty good tutorial on resizing here at DPC and if you use Photoshop, their "Save for Web" is a pretty good way of getting a useful size for competition. Good luck in your future entries in DPC.  You''ve got a good eye and I''m looking forward to seeing more of your work. Alice', '2010-01-30 15:17:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844554, 848822, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Excellent and fun stop-action shot!  This did well scorewise and finished well in a very competitive challenge.  It does seem to me to need a bit more contrast to really make that water "pop" as the saying goes, and perhaps a crop of the foreground rock.  But you did indeed catch this at just the right moment for the photo to have solid impact.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Excellent and fun stop-action shot!  This did well scorewise and finished well in a very competitive challenge.  It does seem to me to need a bit more contrast to really make that water "pop" as the saying goes, and perhaps a crop of the foreground rock.  But you did indeed catch this at just the right moment for the photo to have solid impact. ', '2010-01-30 15:25:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844564, 848888, 61651, '<td valign="top">This is heartbreaking, and a beautiful interpretation of the theory.  I had thought too, that the bisection of the image between black/white, dark/light was symbolic of the transition between life and death.</td>', 'This is heartbreaking, and a beautiful interpretation of the theory.  I had thought too, that the bisection of the image between black/white, dark/light was symbolic of the transition between life and death.', '2010-01-30 15:36:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844589, 848485, 68586, '<td valign="top">Cute looking dog and good attempt to capture him - good dog portraits are a lot harder than they first look. <br/> <br/>Perhaps this shot might have worked better if you had taken off his collar and tags, then got him to sit down and look at you ?<br/> <br/>Oh and try to be aware of the background as well - that broken twig by his muzzle draws the viewers eye away from the main subject.<br/> <br/>A handsome pup though and something has certainly got his attention  :- )<br/> <br/></td>', 'Cute looking dog and good attempt to capture him - good dog portraits are a lot harder than they first look.  Perhaps this shot might have worked better if you had taken off his collar and tags, then got him to sit down and look at you ? Oh and try to be aware of the background as well - that broken twig by his muzzle draws the viewers eye away from the main subject. A handsome pup though and something has certainly got his attention  :- ) ', '2010-01-30 15:53:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844610, 847914, 112828, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>My first impression of this image was a big WOW!  It immediately brought back memories of the Pueblo summer solstice ceremonies in Chaco Canyon.  The bright colors and action, along with that excellent expression and blue eyes just grabs my attention.<br/> <br/>Really nice composition, you have the major attention point directly on a rule-of-thirds line intersection, and you have managed to create an interesting leading line from the group of people trailing off to the upper right corner. <br/> <br/>Your subject matter couldn''t be more interesting (to me).  I just love the action and vibrancy of these people, it looks for all the world as if they were truly having the time of their lives.<br/> <br/>I love the focus here, super sharp on the foreground face, and wonderfully soft everywhere else.. Really, really good way to do this...  Noise is almost non-existent, and the low key exposure is really appealing here (I do like low key, so I''m probably slightly biased)..  <br/> <br/>You have created an excellent image, and it certainly deserves to have finished as well, if not better, than it did.  You hear quite a few people talking about this mysterious thing called impact... Well, this image has it.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  My first impression of this image was a big WOW!  It immediately brought back memories of the Pueblo summer solstice ceremonies in Chaco Canyon.  The bright colors and action, along with that excellent expression and blue eyes just grabs my attention. Really nice composition, you have the major attention point directly on a rule-of-thirds line intersection, and you have managed to create an interesting leading line from the group of people trailing off to the upper right corner.  Your subject matter couldn''t be more interesting (to me).  I just love the action and vibrancy of these people, it looks for all the world as if they were truly having the time of their lives. I love the focus here, super sharp on the foreground face, and wonderfully soft everywhere else.. Really, really good way to do this...  Noise is almost non-existent, and the low key exposure is really appealing here (I do like low key, so I''m probably slightly biased)..   You have created an excellent image, and it certainly deserves to have finished as well, if not better, than it did.  You hear quite a few people talking about this mysterious thing called impact... Well, this image has it. ', '2010-01-30 16:09:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844625, 848776, 39850, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by JimiRose:</b></div><hr/><i> ...He just has really big paws, no camera tricks there!...<br/> <br/>Yes he''s a mongrel of some kind, half jack russell half something with really big paws! He was a rescue dog so we don''t really know. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Acknowledging your note about the super paws<br/> <br/>and rescued - excellent - he is obviously living a good life now and has a fancy cow collar to boot (which I also noticed during voting)</td>', 'Originally posted by JimiRose: ...He just has really big paws, no camera tricks there!... Yes he''s a mongrel of some kind, half jack russell half something with really big paws! He was a rescue dog so we don''t really know.   Acknowledging your note about the super paws and rescued - excellent - he is obviously living a good life now and has a fancy cow collar to boot (which I also noticed during voting)', '2010-01-30 16:34:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(5844635, 848888, 39850, '<td valign="top">I gave this an 8 and really liked the way you split the image into ''two'' - black and white - and the simple, beautiful, natural scene itself, ''transported'' from what you would have seen, into an artistic photograph of a moment in time.</td>', 'I gave this an 8 and really liked the way you split the image into ''two'' - black and white - and the simple, beautiful, natural scene itself, ''transported'' from what you would have seen, into an artistic photograph of a moment in time.', '2010-01-30 16:49:32.000000', NULL, 0),
