(5845607, 847753, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Great location for this shot, just cries out for long exposures and low ISOs and tons of dof...so in that respect you certainly nailed it.  Composition is very pleasing for this land/seascape.  Extra kudos for getting up early for the shot!  And as a proud owner of that lens, I can appreciate its use here :-)<br/> <br/>But...and there is always a but...as a fan and practitioner of very minimal editing, I find it a tad on the heavy side.  I love the shot but would like to see it with less pp, or even sftc.  The water looking silky is fine, but the sky and driftwood do draw my eye, as they just don''t look right to me.<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me with any questions,<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Great location for this shot, just cries out for long exposures and low ISOs and tons of dof...so in that respect you certainly nailed it.  Composition is very pleasing for this land/seascape.  Extra kudos for getting up early for the shot!  And as a proud owner of that lens, I can appreciate its use here :-) But...and there is always a but...as a fan and practitioner of very minimal editing, I find it a tad on the heavy side.  I love the shot but would like to see it with less pp, or even sftc.  The water looking silky is fine, but the sky and driftwood do draw my eye, as they just don''t look right to me. Feel free to PM me with any questions, Susan', '2010-01-31 18:24:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845616, 847831, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Lovely tones of blue here, nice use of the moon for drama and illumination (to an extent anyway).  Like that you did very basic editing, that''s my fave too :-)  I wonder about the high ISO, after all the wall did well for stability.  I would''ve gone down pretty low in ISO, closed down the aperture and left the shutter open a lot longer.<br/> <br/>The comp does look busy, you could almost split this horizontally into two separate shots.  The sets of boats battle it out for your eye.  Almost a little glare off of the sodium lights.<br/> <br/>Overall, not a bad shot, just needs a little more work.  Go forth and shoot!<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me with any questions,<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Lovely tones of blue here, nice use of the moon for drama and illumination (to an extent anyway).  Like that you did very basic editing, that''s my fave too :-)  I wonder about the high ISO, after all the wall did well for stability.  I would''ve gone down pretty low in ISO, closed down the aperture and left the shutter open a lot longer. The comp does look busy, you could almost split this horizontally into two separate shots.  The sets of boats battle it out for your eye.  Almost a little glare off of the sodium lights. Overall, not a bad shot, just needs a little more work.  Go forth and shoot! Feel free to PM me with any questions, Susan', '2010-01-31 18:39:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845635, 847812, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>What are the odds that a professional horsewoman would draw this one to critique? Very interesting stance in the foreground horse, that rested hind leg indicates that he is at ease.  At a guess I would say the b/g horse is a possible successor to the foreground horse, who to my eye looks quite a bit older.  B/g horse looks to be younger and in much better weight.<br/> <br/>Now onto the shot.  Very dramatic pose, setting is amazing, and the camera settings are what I would expect to see in this situation.  Lords-of-the-Rings quality to the shot.  Would love to know if these are feral or domesticated horses.<br/> <br/>Overall, how can I find flaws in a shot that I would have given at least an 8 had I voted?  Even knowing it''s been pp''d, I can''t see it.<br/> <br/>Gorgeous shot.  Great work!<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me with any questions,<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! What are the odds that a professional horsewoman would draw this one to critique? Very interesting stance in the foreground horse, that rested hind leg indicates that he is at ease.  At a guess I would say the b/g horse is a possible successor to the foreground horse, who to my eye looks quite a bit older.  B/g horse looks to be younger and in much better weight. Now onto the shot.  Very dramatic pose, setting is amazing, and the camera settings are what I would expect to see in this situation.  Lords-of-the-Rings quality to the shot.  Would love to know if these are feral or domesticated horses. Overall, how can I find flaws in a shot that I would have given at least an 8 had I voted?  Even knowing it''s been pp''d, I can''t see it. Gorgeous shot.  Great work! Feel free to PM me with any questions, Susan', '2010-01-31 19:23:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845645, 848788, 68357, '<td valign="top">Snillingur !</td>', 'Snillingur !', '2010-01-31 19:34:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845691, 848834, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>First of all, you were shooting a documentary, may have been on set, so may not have been able to leave your location.  Having said that, I can see why so many commenters seem ambivalent this shot.  It does have a bit of a touristy-snapshot feel to it.  Everyone is simply too far away to draw us in, all we see are straining figures hauling on a rope.  The POV is very safe.  Surely if this were a daily occurence at roughly the same time each day, you may have been able to get a more dynamic angle. <br/> <br/>Camera settings seem fine, composition bland though some attempt at rule of thirds. A few very minor nitpicks...some visible sensor dust in upper left corner, and fishing buouys, also detract to a slight but noticable extent.  A lot of cropping could have been done to tighten the shot.  Yes, documentaries and many movies are shot with such a long shot to give a sense of place....but then they generally zoom in on the action pretty fast to keep the audience engaged.<br/> <br/>So that''s what I suggest you keep in mind for now.  Get the action!<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me with any questions,<br/> <br/>Susan<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! First of all, you were shooting a documentary, may have been on set, so may not have been able to leave your location.  Having said that, I can see why so many commenters seem ambivalent this shot.  It does have a bit of a touristy-snapshot feel to it.  Everyone is simply too far away to draw us in, all we see are straining figures hauling on a rope.  The POV is very safe.  Surely if this were a daily occurence at roughly the same time each day, you may have been able to get a more dynamic angle.  Camera settings seem fine, composition bland though some attempt at rule of thirds. A few very minor nitpicks...some visible sensor dust in upper left corner, and fishing buouys, also detract to a slight but noticable extent.  A lot of cropping could have been done to tighten the shot.  Yes, documentaries and many movies are shot with such a long shot to give a sense of place....but then they generally zoom in on the action pretty fast to keep the audience engaged. So that''s what I suggest you keep in mind for now.  Get the action! Feel free to PM me with any questions, Susan', '2010-01-31 20:41:32.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(5845703, 847173, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I''m probably not the best to critique this shot as I am not a "foodie", but based on your comments received during the challenge, you''ve pleased many of those folks who do like food shots, so that''s good!  To me, this looks pretty well lit although there are a few reflections that a softer light might have prevented.  Colors are good and seem very natural.  The only suggestion I''d make, and not sure how you do it, is to ensure the spoon is clean above the gravy line, if that makes sense.  And good job catching the gravy as it drips!<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! I''m probably not the best to critique this shot as I am not a "foodie", but based on your comments received during the challenge, you''ve pleased many of those folks who do like food shots, so that''s good!  To me, this looks pretty well lit although there are a few reflections that a softer light might have prevented.  Colors are good and seem very natural.  The only suggestion I''d make, and not sure how you do it, is to ensure the spoon is clean above the gravy line, if that makes sense.  And good job catching the gravy as it drips! ', '2010-01-31 20:51:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845710, 848911, 111167, '<td valign="top">Wow!!</td>', 'Wow!!', '2010-01-31 20:57:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845716, 847562, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>A lovely scene here and I can almost hear the water as it goes by.  You did indeed get very good clarity and detail with your shot and the composition feels fairly well balanced to me.  I wonder if you did try other compositions as well?  One focused more on the rocks and water, then one focused more on the sky which includes the tops of the trees?  I try to remember to do this, but usually forget (and really, we''re not wasting film!)  The light here is nice and soft, nothing seems over or underexposed.  The shot does seem to be just a bit "flat"; all digital files from DSLRs require some bit of tweaking, and in this case I think a bit of curves and/or some additional contrast would not only help define the water a bit more, but also bring out that light on the trees and mist you''re trying to emphasize here.  It may be worth experimenting a bit - take it FAR beyond what you think is "natural" as far as contrast and saturation, for instance, then start pulling it back and see what you think.<br/> <br/>I see you haven''t entered many challenges - hope you''ll enter a few more this year!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! A lovely scene here and I can almost hear the water as it goes by.  You did indeed get very good clarity and detail with your shot and the composition feels fairly well balanced to me.  I wonder if you did try other compositions as well?  One focused more on the rocks and water, then one focused more on the sky which includes the tops of the trees?  I try to remember to do this, but usually forget (and really, we''re not wasting film!)  The light here is nice and soft, nothing seems over or underexposed.  The shot does seem to be just a bit "flat"; all digital files from DSLRs require some bit of tweaking, and in this case I think a bit of curves and/or some additional contrast would not only help define the water a bit more, but also bring out that light on the trees and mist you''re trying to emphasize here.  It may be worth experimenting a bit - take it FAR beyond what you think is "natural" as far as contrast and saturation, for instance, then start pulling it back and see what you think. I see you haven''t entered many challenges - hope you''ll enter a few more this year!', '2010-01-31 21:03:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845718, 847562, 30982, '<td valign="top">P.S.  Just noticed you do have another one in voting - good to see!  (I can tell because it says you''ve entered five challenges but only four are showing on your profile page right now.)</td>', 'P.S.  Just noticed you do have another one in voting - good to see!  (I can tell because it says you''ve entered five challenges but only four are showing on your profile page right now.)', '2010-01-31 21:05:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845732, 848367, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB</b> <br/> <br/>Wow, am I glad I got to see this one.  We are nearly at rollover, and I don''t know how much longer this batch will be up.  In a word:  I really, really like your image.<br/> <br/>Yes, DPCers tend to grade on a curve, and there was some stiff competition in this Best of 2009 Challenge.  Your comments already tell you you were over-saturated, under-saturated, and on and on.  It''s a perfectly adequate image and a fun one to enjoy for its splash of color.  <br/> <br/>The one nit I might pick, (and that''s why I''m here) is the crop.  If it was mine, I might remove the left section that has the few stray petals in the upper corner.  this tightens up the composition and saves a viewer from trying to fill in the missing pieces.  <br/> <br/>I love the explosion of color and the shine on the undeveloped sections of the seedpod.  <br/> <br/>Fun image.  (I also took the opportunity to enjoy a bit of your portfolio - there is surely some very nice work in there.)<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB  Wow, am I glad I got to see this one.  We are nearly at rollover, and I don''t know how much longer this batch will be up.  In a word:  I really, really like your image. Yes, DPCers tend to grade on a curve, and there was some stiff competition in this Best of 2009 Challenge.  Your comments already tell you you were over-saturated, under-saturated, and on and on.  It''s a perfectly adequate image and a fun one to enjoy for its splash of color.   The one nit I might pick, (and that''s why I''m here) is the crop.  If it was mine, I might remove the left section that has the few stray petals in the upper corner.  this tightens up the composition and saves a viewer from trying to fill in the missing pieces.   I love the explosion of color and the shine on the undeveloped sections of the seedpod.   Fun image.  (I also took the opportunity to enjoy a bit of your portfolio - there is surely some very nice work in there.) Alice', '2010-01-31 21:17:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845738, 848858, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB</b> <br/> <br/>Congratulations on a very good image that scored well in this demanding "Best of 2009" Challenge.  <br/> <br/>You used the Topaz program gently and well;  your composition was spot on.  Your many comments and your fine score tell you that this image is a good one.<br/> <br/>Therefore, since "there is nothing to critique here" I shall move on to the next image in the queue.</td>', 'GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB  Congratulations on a very good image that scored well in this demanding "Best of 2009" Challenge.   You used the Topaz program gently and well;  your composition was spot on.  Your many comments and your fine score tell you that this image is a good one. Therefore, since "there is nothing to critique here" I shall move on to the next image in the queue.', '2010-01-31 21:22:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845748, 848001, 14476, '<td valign="top">GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>Congratulations on a fine image and a fine finish in this demanding competition. With a 6.56 finish, you don''t need much of a critique from the likes of me!  Aren''t you glad you took that class?<br/> <br/>This review also gave me the chance to look at some of your other work. I must say your <b>Predator</b> shot was one I admired a great deal and still wonder how you got that underwing detail.<br/> <br/>Well okay, since there is ''nothing to critique here'' I guess I must move along to the next image in the queue.<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB Congratulations on a fine image and a fine finish in this demanding competition. With a 6.56 finish, you don''t need much of a critique from the likes of me!  Aren''t you glad you took that class? This review also gave me the chance to look at some of your other work. I must say your Predator shot was one I admired a great deal and still wonder how you got that underwing detail. Well okay, since there is ''nothing to critique here'' I guess I must move along to the next image in the queue. Alice', '2010-01-31 21:31:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845767, 848507, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB</b> <br/> <br/>Sometimes I just love this job.  Yeah, you got a low score, so what.  this is an image that means a great deal to you and your model is beautifully rendered.  yeah, the background isn''t so hot, but I rather think you just didn''t see it.  The figure is lovely and powerful and that drew your attention and you handled it with care.<br/> <br/>While here, I looked through your portfolio a little and found other images that tell a story.  rather than looking for ''photographic excellence'' whatever that is, you seem to go for the story.  (your picture with the  flower &amp; keys is an example) <br/> <br/>Well, you''ve done it again.  No, it didn''t resonate with the voters, but who cares.  You know why you took it and entered it, and that''s what counts.<br/> <br/>I look forward to your next entry.  You bet.<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB  Sometimes I just love this job.  Yeah, you got a low score, so what.  this is an image that means a great deal to you and your model is beautifully rendered.  yeah, the background isn''t so hot, but I rather think you just didn''t see it.  The figure is lovely and powerful and that drew your attention and you handled it with care. While here, I looked through your portfolio a little and found other images that tell a story.  rather than looking for ''photographic excellence'' whatever that is, you seem to go for the story.  (your picture with the  flower & keys is an example)  Well, you''ve done it again.  No, it didn''t resonate with the voters, but who cares.  You know why you took it and entered it, and that''s what counts. I look forward to your next entry.  You bet. Alice', '2010-01-31 21:42:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845768, 847116, 100831, '<td valign="top">this is just unbelievable - so sharp and such rich colors.</td>', 'this is just unbelievable - so sharp and such rich colors.', '2010-01-31 21:46:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845787, 847252, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>''Tis almost a bit unfair that I drew this one, as I loved this in voting, and still do.  You were in my top tier of vote getters in this challenge - as I said before, I love the quietness of this.  There''s a peacefulness here, an isolation.  I see that another commenter interpreted it as lonely - and it could be seen that way as well.<br/> <br/>Many didn''t like the noise in the photo, others did.  Hard to please everyone when it comes to digital noise.  I think it works well for this photograph but I see from your notes that you don''t like it.  You could try going to the other extreme here - use a good noise removal tool (Neat Image, for instance) or a "glow" kind of process to completely soften everything up.  Would render a completely different feel.  You could also try converting to monochrome (but then I am a B&amp;W person at heart, so keep that in mind when weighing that option!)<br/> <br/>Pleased to see that after having been a user at DPC for quite awhile you''re taking a more active role in challenges - I look forward to seeing more of your entries in the future!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! ''Tis almost a bit unfair that I drew this one, as I loved this in voting, and still do.  You were in my top tier of vote getters in this challenge - as I said before, I love the quietness of this.  There''s a peacefulness here, an isolation.  I see that another commenter interpreted it as lonely - and it could be seen that way as well. Many didn''t like the noise in the photo, others did.  Hard to please everyone when it comes to digital noise.  I think it works well for this photograph but I see from your notes that you don''t like it.  You could try going to the other extreme here - use a good noise removal tool (Neat Image, for instance) or a "glow" kind of process to completely soften everything up.  Would render a completely different feel.  You could also try converting to monochrome (but then I am a B&W person at heart, so keep that in mind when weighing that option!) Pleased to see that after having been a user at DPC for quite awhile you''re taking a more active role in challenges - I look forward to seeing more of your entries in the future!', '2010-01-31 21:57:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845799, 848728, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>It seems I keep drawing the photographs that I particularly loved during voting, which of course makes it hard for me to "critique".  I really cannot offer any possible "improvements" to this.  It is amazing it its grace, its scope, its majesty.  And the colors are exactly right, by the way, regardless of what was really there.  Note that your commenters really loved this - any time you can move a lot of people to stop and comment, you''ve done something right!<br/> <br/>Please continue to share your vision as you see it.  And make sure you have that strap around your neck when shooting pictures out of the airplane window.  :-)</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! It seems I keep drawing the photographs that I particularly loved during voting, which of course makes it hard for me to "critique".  I really cannot offer any possible "improvements" to this.  It is amazing it its grace, its scope, its majesty.  And the colors are exactly right, by the way, regardless of what was really there.  Note that your commenters really loved this - any time you can move a lot of people to stop and comment, you''ve done something right! Please continue to share your vision as you see it.  And make sure you have that strap around your neck when shooting pictures out of the airplane window.  :-)', '2010-01-31 22:05:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845839, 847866, 9462, '<td valign="top">This is so awesome.</td>', 'This is so awesome.', '2010-01-31 22:31:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845893, 848682, 103142, '<td valign="top">Good day! You''re the recipient of a "critique club" critique! So feel free to take this with a grain of salt :D<br/> <br/>First Impression: I''m glad that I received this one to critique -- It''s a very well done shot that I enjoyed when voting, but something just wasn''t right. What a great opportunity to explore this one further.<br/> <br/>Composition: The biggest problem with this image is the disconnect between to the two people. With your title of "not interested", it''s fine and perfectly acceptable that he''s disconnected/uninterested. But for the picture to be really intriguing, there should be some interaction. If she was only looking at him, putting a hand out to him, somehow trying to catch his eye, then there would be so much more of a story. Right now it is two separate pictures. I do agree with an earlier commenter, he''d be much more interesting by himself. At this moment, she''s just a distraction. If she was working into the story, that would be a different matter.<br/> <br/>Subject: As above: the man is very interesting, the woman doesn''t add much. Wonderful spot, though!<br/> <br/>Technical: You did the technicals exceedingly well. I like the chosen angle, the lighting and processing are very nice.<br/> <br/>Final thoughts: I gave this a 7 in the voting, I did find it intriguing, however the man by himself would have scored an 8 or 9 from me.</td>', 'Good day! You''re the recipient of a "critique club" critique! So feel free to take this with a grain of salt :D First Impression: I''m glad that I received this one to critique -- It''s a very well done shot that I enjoyed when voting, but something just wasn''t right. What a great opportunity to explore this one further. Composition: The biggest problem with this image is the disconnect between to the two people. With your title of "not interested", it''s fine and perfectly acceptable that he''s disconnected/uninterested. But for the picture to be really intriguing, there should be some interaction. If she was only looking at him, putting a hand out to him, somehow trying to catch his eye, then there would be so much more of a story. Right now it is two separate pictures. I do agree with an earlier commenter, he''d be much more interesting by himself. At this moment, she''s just a distraction. If she was working into the story, that would be a different matter. Subject: As above: the man is very interesting, the woman doesn''t add much. Wonderful spot, though! Technical: You did the technicals exceedingly well. I like the chosen angle, the lighting and processing are very nice. Final thoughts: I gave this a 7 in the voting, I did find it intriguing, however the man by himself would have scored an 8 or 9 from me.', '2010-01-31 23:04:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845902, 848278, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB</b> <br/> <br/>What a splendid image.  I am so pleased to have the chance to look at it and enjoy it once more.<br/> <br/>You had many comments and they told you want might be ''improved'' with this image.  You also have comments telling you what a fine image it is.  <br/> <br/>Sure, a larger size would have been helpful, and you will probably figure out how to achieve the appropriate size for DPC entries soon.  There is a tutorial here on the site that may help.<br/> <br/>Your sunrise/sunset shot is so appealing.  The perfectly captured grains spilling out of the dish, the silhouette treatment, all are powerful and emotive.   I have visited your wonderful country and know how exciting and colorful the sun at the horizon can be.  You treated it carefully and carried it off nicely.  <br/> <br/>I look forward to seeing more entries from you.  When I peeked at your portfolio I noticed you also enjoy bright color photography.  Maybe we will see some of those soon as well.<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB  What a splendid image.  I am so pleased to have the chance to look at it and enjoy it once more. You had many comments and they told you want might be ''improved'' with this image.  You also have comments telling you what a fine image it is.   Sure, a larger size would have been helpful, and you will probably figure out how to achieve the appropriate size for DPC entries soon.  There is a tutorial here on the site that may help. Your sunrise/sunset shot is so appealing.  The perfectly captured grains spilling out of the dish, the silhouette treatment, all are powerful and emotive.   I have visited your wonderful country and know how exciting and colorful the sun at the horizon can be.  You treated it carefully and carried it off nicely.   I look forward to seeing more entries from you.  When I peeked at your portfolio I noticed you also enjoy bright color photography.  Maybe we will see some of those soon as well. Alice', '2010-01-31 23:15:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845917, 848446, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I liked this in voting - a pleasing array of architecture and texture with some good color thrown in.  And it made me just a touch homesick for Europe.  While some may see this as a "ruin" or "history", I''m quite sure it is simply someone''s home, and they probably don''t even notice the history of the place.  My only nit is that from a composition standpoint, it is not perfectly centered, but it is not far enough off center to be well balanced.  I''m wondering if a stronger angle or perhaps a dead-center comp might have been a bit stronger.  But that''s neither here nor there at this point.  I suspect this brings back fond memories of a place visited and that alone is a great reason for a memorable photograph!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! I liked this in voting - a pleasing array of architecture and texture with some good color thrown in.  And it made me just a touch homesick for Europe.  While some may see this as a "ruin" or "history", I''m quite sure it is simply someone''s home, and they probably don''t even notice the history of the place.  My only nit is that from a composition standpoint, it is not perfectly centered, but it is not far enough off center to be well balanced.  I''m wondering if a stronger angle or perhaps a dead-center comp might have been a bit stronger.  But that''s neither here nor there at this point.  I suspect this brings back fond memories of a place visited and that alone is a great reason for a memorable photograph!', '2010-01-31 23:22:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845920, 848336, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB</b> <br/> <br/>WooHoo - when I saw this one I gasped.  What a combination!  the bright reds of the rose and the stark blue/white of the gemstones.<br/> <br/>There ''s a lot to like in this push-pull super sharp image. It is very well photographed and elicited a large amount of commenting from our viewers.  what''s so much fun about the comments is that they run the gambit, so you can just pick ''n choose the ones you ''like.''<br/> <br/>I do agree with the person who suggested that the ring shank color (most of it turned red) should have retained its original color.  That is the only, well, false, note in the entire composition. <br/> <br/>You''re good and I enjoyed a peak at your portfolio. (One of the perks of this job is looking at the work of people you might not otherwise get to)<br/>Keep up the good work and I look forward to seeing more of your images. <br/> <br/></td>', 'GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB  WooHoo - when I saw this one I gasped.  What a combination!  the bright reds of the rose and the stark blue/white of the gemstones. There ''s a lot to like in this push-pull super sharp image. It is very well photographed and elicited a large amount of commenting from our viewers.  what''s so much fun about the comments is that they run the gambit, so you can just pick ''n choose the ones you ''like.'' I do agree with the person who suggested that the ring shank color (most of it turned red) should have retained its original color.  That is the only, well, false, note in the entire composition.  You''re good and I enjoyed a peak at your portfolio. (One of the perks of this job is looking at the work of people you might not otherwise get to)Keep up the good work and I look forward to seeing more of your images.  ', '2010-01-31 23:24:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845933, 848481, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Ha!  I''m not gonna "critique" anything that came in 4th of 400+ in a Best of challenge with a 7+ score and a whole slew of comments both during and after the challenge.  Awesome!  Congrats!!  And it does indeed appear this photographic trip was well worth the effort.  Nice job, Janine!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Ha!  I''m not gonna "critique" anything that came in 4th of 400+ in a Best of challenge with a 7+ score and a whole slew of comments both during and after the challenge.  Awesome!  Congrats!!  And it does indeed appear this photographic trip was well worth the effort.  Nice job, Janine!', '2010-01-31 23:27:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(5845969, 848794, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Interesting that in the random draw I keep getting shots that I''ve already commented on....  Anyway, what I said after the challenge still stands.  I just noticed your score - I would have expected it to have been higher, actually.  From a technical standpoint, *I* like where the focus is just fine but there may be some who''d prefer it srtonger on the fly and would prefer the whole fly.  But that would change the context of the picture, to me.  Color is excellent, and as I said before the composition creates a nifty little world.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Interesting that in the random draw I keep getting shots that I''ve already commented on....  Anyway, what I said after the challenge still stands.  I just noticed your score - I would have expected it to have been higher, actually.  From a technical standpoint, *I* like where the focus is just fine but there may be some who''d prefer it srtonger on the fly and would prefer the whole fly.  But that would change the context of the picture, to me.  Color is excellent, and as I said before the composition creates a nifty little world.', '2010-01-31 23:38:47.000000', NULL, 0),
