(5846704, 847476, 105457, '<td valign="top">?Hola Roberto, que tal? :)<br/> <br/>Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Regarding your photo, it is important that you realize that the score does not always reflect the quality of the picture. Having said that:<br/> <br/><b>The scene</b> <br/>Very appealing in it''s whole, the composition is very good. The slight tilt is not very usual in landscapes, but it might have worked to your advantage. It gives a sense of water flow to the right of the picture.<br/> <br/><b>Processing</b> <br/>Looking at your original, you managed to process it very well. It is definitely not eye candy, and probably that''s why it placed where it did.<br/>The high vignetting in the original, plus the post processing of the shot make it look like a pinhole camera shot. If it was the intention, you succeeded brilliantly! <br/> <br/>The sepia tones make it very vintage, postcard like. <br/>It is indeed dark, and in some computer screens it would be too dark for someone to distinguish the features, but the style you chose was that in fact.<br/> <br/>The border was a good choice, it does not detract from the picture.<br/> <br/>If you have any questions feel free to <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/messaging.php?action=compose&amp;USER_ID=105457" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">contact me</a>.<br/> <br/>Regards,<br/>Joao</td>', '?Hola Roberto, que tal? :) Greetings from the Critique Club! Regarding your photo, it is important that you realize that the score does not always reflect the quality of the picture. Having said that: The scene Very appealing in it''s whole, the composition is very good. The slight tilt is not very usual in landscapes, but it might have worked to your advantage. It gives a sense of water flow to the right of the picture. Processing Looking at your original, you managed to process it very well. It is definitely not eye candy, and probably that''s why it placed where it did.The high vignetting in the original, plus the post processing of the shot make it look like a pinhole camera shot. If it was the intention, you succeeded brilliantly!  The sepia tones make it very vintage, postcard like. It is indeed dark, and in some computer screens it would be too dark for someone to distinguish the features, but the style you chose was that in fact. The border was a good choice, it does not detract from the picture. If you have any questions feel free to contact me. Regards,Joao', '2010-02-01 05:43:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(5846864, 847130, 105457, '<td valign="top">Hi Rune, greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Thank you for participating in the Best of 2009 challenge.<br/>First of all, don''t be disencouraged by your placing, you still received a 5,5 in a photo, and this challenge had a lot of good photos to look at.<br/> <br/>Regarding your photo:<br/> <br/>Lighting<br/>The most well processed parts of this picture are the sky and the truck. They have been very improved by the HDR treatment.<br/>The mixture between yellow of the sky and blue of the truck make for a pleasing balance.<br/>The rest of the picture is a bit dark, and probably some users had problems with some displays, some notebooks do display it very dark, and it could have worked against you.<br/> <br/>Composition<br/>There''s one thing that probably kept your shot from scoring higher, and that is the branches in front.<br/>I''m not a supporter of damaging the scene when you shoot, but by the looks of the surroundings if you would have taken out the branches in front, the truck would be clearly visible, making it the central point. This way, it''s just something more in the landscape. Also, a shot from a lower perspective could have got you more sky, it''s very beautiful. Still, the overall composition is correct.<br/> <br/>Processing<br/>As said before, a bit brighter would have made it more acessible to most users. Anyway, good exposure at Photomatix.<br/> <br/>If you have any questions feel free to <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/messaging.php?action=compose&amp;USER_ID=105457" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">contact me</a>.<br/> <br/>Regards,<br/>Joao<br/></td>', 'Hi Rune, greetings from the Critique Club! Thank you for participating in the Best of 2009 challenge.First of all, don''t be disencouraged by your placing, you still received a 5,5 in a photo, and this challenge had a lot of good photos to look at. Regarding your photo: LightingThe most well processed parts of this picture are the sky and the truck. They have been very improved by the HDR treatment.The mixture between yellow of the sky and blue of the truck make for a pleasing balance.The rest of the picture is a bit dark, and probably some users had problems with some displays, some notebooks do display it very dark, and it could have worked against you. CompositionThere''s one thing that probably kept your shot from scoring higher, and that is the branches in front.I''m not a supporter of damaging the scene when you shoot, but by the looks of the surroundings if you would have taken out the branches in front, the truck would be clearly visible, making it the central point. This way, it''s just something more in the landscape. Also, a shot from a lower perspective could have got you more sky, it''s very beautiful. Still, the overall composition is correct. ProcessingAs said before, a bit brighter would have made it more acessible to most users. Anyway, good exposure at Photomatix. If you have any questions feel free to contact me. Regards,Joao', '2010-02-01 06:46:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(5847164, 848927, 51269, '<td valign="top">I''m glad you kept it- very serene &amp; lovely.</td>', 'I''m glad you kept it- very serene & lovely.', '2010-02-01 09:54:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(5847172, 848419, 51269, '<td valign="top">Cool image but I don''t think it''s your "best" of 2009- hard to choose ! You do good work my dear :)</td>', 'Cool image but I don''t think it''s your "best" of 2009- hard to choose ! You do good work my dear :)', '2010-02-01 09:57:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(5847294, 848877, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>What a find in the middle of Illinois!  I too, would have thought these trees would be located on the edges of Florida!  <br/> <br/>It was a good find and you made the most of it.  Your many comments tell you that there were many interpretations of your handling of this scene.<br/> <br/>I guess I am on "the side" of those who think it is a touch over-saturated and thus tends towards harshness.  The Hue/Saturation sliders in Photoshop are helpful and by going through all of them just a little at a time, it is frequently possible to build up to an optimum presentation. I put your very fine image on my  own computer and (based on the date taken) adjusted the colors to be a bit more cold.  That seemed to take the harshness away.<br/> <br/>this is just a thought, of course.  You are the complete judge of how your work presents.  <br/> <br/>Keep up the good work.  You say you are new to this.  Lots of practice and playtime will make you an expert in no time and I look forward to seeing more of your work.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  What a find in the middle of Illinois!  I too, would have thought these trees would be located on the edges of Florida!   It was a good find and you made the most of it.  Your many comments tell you that there were many interpretations of your handling of this scene. I guess I am on "the side" of those who think it is a touch over-saturated and thus tends towards harshness.  The Hue/Saturation sliders in Photoshop are helpful and by going through all of them just a little at a time, it is frequently possible to build up to an optimum presentation. I put your very fine image on my  own computer and (based on the date taken) adjusted the colors to be a bit more cold.  That seemed to take the harshness away. this is just a thought, of course.  You are the complete judge of how your work presents.   Keep up the good work.  You say you are new to this.  Lots of practice and playtime will make you an expert in no time and I look forward to seeing more of your work.', '2010-02-01 11:22:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(5847304, 848644, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB</b> <br/> <br/>Congratulations on yet another fine animal portrait from what appears to be your favorite zoo.<br/> <br/>It shows that you know your critters and shoot them with understanding and compassion and do it very well.  <br/> <br/>You have many fine constructive comments and don''t really need a critique with that good score of yours.  So add my congratulations to the list and I''ll say thanks for the opportunity to romp through a little of your portfolio before heading on to the next image in the Critique club queue.<br/> <br/>Alice<br/>:-))</td>', 'GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB  Congratulations on yet another fine animal portrait from what appears to be your favorite zoo. It shows that you know your critters and shoot them with understanding and compassion and do it very well.   You have many fine constructive comments and don''t really need a critique with that good score of yours.  So add my congratulations to the list and I''ll say thanks for the opportunity to romp through a little of your portfolio before heading on to the next image in the Critique club queue. Alice:-))', '2010-02-01 11:30:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(5847322, 848162, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB</b> <br/> <br/>Well, <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=113424" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">jeffreype123</a>, if your intention was to make the voters smile, you succeeded beyond belief.  If, in addition, your intention was to score well, well you have time for that later.<br/> <br/>For the record, this is an excellent macro shot.  Did I know toes have (er)toeprints, like fingerprints?  Well, I do now.  Nice placement in the frame (I can''t believe I''m saying this) and good colors.  The green thingee in the upper right bothered me a little at first, but then I thought, "Why not!"<br/> <br/>Would I hang it on my wall?  Um, I will think about it.  Is it amusing and fun and all that.  Oh, yes.  <br/> <br/>So, thanks for submitting it to the "Best of 2009" Challenge, keep your sense of humor, and I <b>DO</b> look forward to seeing more of your work.<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'GREETINGS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB  Well,  jeffreype123, if your intention was to make the voters smile, you succeeded beyond belief.  If, in addition, your intention was to score well, well you have time for that later. For the record, this is an excellent macro shot.  Did I know toes have (er)toeprints, like fingerprints?  Well, I do now.  Nice placement in the frame (I can''t believe I''m saying this) and good colors.  The green thingee in the upper right bothered me a little at first, but then I thought, "Why not!" Would I hang it on my wall?  Um, I will think about it.  Is it amusing and fun and all that.  Oh, yes.   So, thanks for submitting it to the "Best of 2009" Challenge, keep your sense of humor, and I DO look forward to seeing more of your work. Alice', '2010-02-01 11:38:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(5847336, 847534, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>GREETIngS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB</b> <br/> <br/>Oh, I am SO envious.   You were in Paris last year and I was not.  Not only that, but you captured a great street scene.<br/> <br/>Interesting.  You finished just about in the middle of the pack in this demanding "Best of 2009" Challenge. And that is just about the way your comments split on your treatment of this scene.  <br/> <br/>HDR, Topaz.  Like?  Don''t Like?   That is the question.  <br/> <br/>My personal opinion is that you have taken this excellent image almost to the point of illustration and if I was lucky enough to call it mine, I''d probably do it more in a photographic way.  But this fine image is yours, not mine.  <br/> <br/>You had fun with it and it shows very nicely.  that''s what counts.  Keep up the good work.  <br/> <br/>Alice<br/>:-))<br/></td>', 'GREETIngS FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB  Oh, I am SO envious.   You were in Paris last year and I was not.  Not only that, but you captured a great street scene. Interesting.  You finished just about in the middle of the pack in this demanding "Best of 2009" Challenge. And that is just about the way your comments split on your treatment of this scene.   HDR, Topaz.  Like?  Don''t Like?   That is the question.   My personal opinion is that you have taken this excellent image almost to the point of illustration and if I was lucky enough to call it mine, I''d probably do it more in a photographic way.  But this fine image is yours, not mine.   You had fun with it and it shows very nicely.  that''s what counts.  Keep up the good work.   Alice:-))', '2010-02-01 11:47:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(5847388, 848848, 105457, '<td valign="top">Hi Brandi, greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>First reaction I had when I saw your photo was: "Wow, there''s so much details on the fur, it''s amazing!"<br/>It''s a shame that it place here it did, but anyway it was a 5.78 score, not too bad.<br/> <br/>Lighting<br/>Great use of natural lighting, the shadows are not distractive, and the subject is very well lit.<br/> <br/>Composition<br/>Although a crop at the top would bring the feline into a more central position, it would make it a square crop, and it might be too tight, I do think that you choose correctly in this manner.<br/> <br/>Processing<br/>As said already below, since it was an advanced edit challenge, some brightening / dodging could have been used on the eyes / face to make it a little bolder.<br/>The sharpening is amazing and it''s what really brings out the picture.<br/>The problem with this scene is that you don''t have that much contrast between the puma and the background; one choice could have been to slightly desaturate the background, so the subject would stand out more. Also lighting up the puma would detach it from the bg pretty well.<br/> <br/>Overall, it''s a photo that really makes an impression, and at 1/60 you had a very steady hand (or a very sturdy tripod :)<br/> <br/>If you have any questions feel free to <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/messaging.php?action=compose&amp;USER_ID=105457" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">contact me</a>.<br/> <br/>Regards,<br/>Joao<br/></td>', 'Hi Brandi, greetings from the Critique Club! First reaction I had when I saw your photo was: "Wow, there''s so much details on the fur, it''s amazing!"It''s a shame that it place here it did, but anyway it was a 5.78 score, not too bad. LightingGreat use of natural lighting, the shadows are not distractive, and the subject is very well lit. CompositionAlthough a crop at the top would bring the feline into a more central position, it would make it a square crop, and it might be too tight, I do think that you choose correctly in this manner. ProcessingAs said already below, since it was an advanced edit challenge, some brightening / dodging could have been used on the eyes / face to make it a little bolder.The sharpening is amazing and it''s what really brings out the picture.The problem with this scene is that you don''t have that much contrast between the puma and the background; one choice could have been to slightly desaturate the background, so the subject would stand out more. Also lighting up the puma would detach it from the bg pretty well. Overall, it''s a photo that really makes an impression, and at 1/60 you had a very steady hand (or a very sturdy tripod :) If you have any questions feel free to contact me. Regards,Joao', '2010-02-01 12:10:27.000000', NULL, 0),
